            <div class="text-slate-700 text-[15.5px] leading-relaxed space-y-4">
              <p>
                Yes—and for many discerning travelers, this is the single best justification for booking a private tour.
              </p>
              <p>
                The Cu Chi complex is divided into two distinct visiting sectors:
              </p>
              <ul class="list-disc pl-5 space-y-2 text-slate-600 text-sm">
                <li><strong>Ben Dinh (~50 km from District 1):</strong> The closer sector, originally used by the Cu Chi District Party Committee. Because it saves roughly 40 minutes of driving each way, it hosts the vast majority of shared group buses, tour coaches, and budget travelers. Passages here have been substantially widened, and trails can become congested during peak morning hours.</li>
                <li><strong>Ben Duoc (~70 km from District 1):</strong> The regional headquarters of Military Region 4 and the Saigon–Gia Dinh Party Committee. Set within a tranquil, sprawling forested park, Ben Duoc preserves a more authentic scale, features fewer crowds, and includes the solemn Ben Duoc Memorial Temple.</li>
              </ul>
              <p>
                Because group bus companies prioritize tight turnarounds and fuel efficiency, finding a shared group tour that visits Ben Duoc is difficult. Most bus itineraries simply take everyone to Ben Dinh.
              </p>
              <p>
                With a private tour, visiting Ben Duoc is effortless. You simply request Ben Duoc when booking. While the drive takes an additional 30 to 45 minutes each way, the reward is an atmospheric, unhurried visit surrounded by native rubber trees and bird calls rather than hundreds of cruise passengers and megaphone announcements.
              </p>
